.TH "LOGJETD" "1" "March 2026" "" ""
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
logjetd - OTLP ingest, \f[C].logjet\f[R] storage, replay, and file
blasting daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[C]logjetd\f[R] [\f[C]serve\f[R]] [\f[C]-c\f[R]|\f[C]--config\f[R]
\f[I]path\f[R]]
.PP
\f[C]logjetd\f[R] \f[C]inspect\f[R] \f[I]path\f[R]
.PP
\f[C]logjetd\f[R] \f[C]replay\f[R] [\f[C]-c\f[R]|\f[C]--config\f[R]
\f[I]path\f[R]] \f[C]--path\f[R] \f[I]dir\f[R] \f[C]--name\f[R]
\f[I]base.logjet\f[R] \f[C]--dest\f[R] \f[I]url-or-host:port\f[R]
.PP
\f[C]logjetd\f[R] \f[C]bridge\f[R] [\f[C]-c\f[R]|\f[C]--config\f[R]
\f[I]path\f[R]] \f[C]--source\f[R] \f[I]host:port\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\f[C]logjetd\f[R] is a small telemetry daemon for constrained systems.
.PP
It can:
.IP \[bu] 2
accept OTLP/HTTP log batches on \f[C]POST /v1/logs\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
accept OTLP/gRPC log batches on the standard
\f[C]LogsService/Export\f[R] endpoint
.IP \[bu] 2
store raw OTLP protobuf payloads either in memory or in append-only
\f[C].logjet\f[R] files
.IP \[bu] 2
expose a replay listener for downstream consumers over the current
internal wire protocol
.IP \[bu] 2
connect to another \f[C]logjetd\f[R] replay listener and forward backlog
plus live records into an OTLP/HTTP collector
.IP \[bu] 2
inspect \f[C].logjet\f[R] files and directories
.IP \[bu] 2
replay stored \f[C].logjet\f[R] files into an OTLP/HTTP collector as a
one-shot operation
.PP
The daemon is designed for cheap hardware, limited RAM, unreliable
storage, and intermittent downstream connectivity.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS serve
.PP
Run the daemon using configuration loaded from
\f[C]/etc/logjet.conf\f[R] by default, or from the file passed with
\f[C]-c\f[R] or \f[C]--config\f[R].
.PP
If no explicit command is given, \f[C]serve\f[R] is the default.
.PP
Current serve behavior:
.IP \[bu] 2
OTLP/HTTP ingest is supported with \f[C]ingest.protocol: otlp-http\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
OTLP/gRPC ingest is supported with \f[C]ingest.protocol: otlp-grpc\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
a replay listener socket is exposed for downstream clients
.IP \[bu] 2
replay listener traffic currently uses the internal framed protocol, not
OTLP egress
.SS bridge
.PP
Connect to another \f[C]logjetd\f[R] replay listener, drain retained
backlog, stay attached for live records, and forward OTLP log payloads
to the configured collector.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
logjetd --config ./logjetd.conf bridge --source 10.0.0.15:7002
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
If \f[C]--source\f[R] is omitted, bridge uses \f[C]upstream.replay\f[R]
from configuration.
.SS inspect
.PP
Inspect a \f[C].logjet\f[R] file or a directory containing
\f[C].logjet\f[R] files.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
logjetd inspect /var/lib/logjet
\f[R]
.fi
.SS replay
.PP
Read ordered \f[C].logjet\f[R] files from a directory and blast the
stored OTLP log payloads into an OTLP/HTTP collector.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
logjetd replay --path /var/logs --name bofh.logjet --dest http://127.0.0.1:4318/v1/logs
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Replay order is:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]bofh.logjet\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]bofh-1.logjet\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]bofh-2.logjet\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
and so on
.PP
Replay is immediate and does not preserve original timing.
If \f[C]--dest\f[R] is omitted, replay uses \f[C]collector.url\f[R] from
configuration.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS \f[C]-c\f[R], \f[C]--config\f[R] \f[I]path\f[R]
.PP
Load configuration from \f[I]path\f[R] instead of
\f[C]/etc/logjet.conf\f[R].
.SS \f[C]-h\f[R], \f[C]--help\f[R]
.PP
Print usage information.
.SS \f[C]--path\f[R] \f[I]dir\f[R]
.PP
Directory containing \f[C].logjet\f[R] files for replay.
.PP
Used only with the \f[C]replay\f[R] command.
.SS \f[C]--name\f[R] \f[I]base.logjet\f[R]
.PP
Base file name used to locate replay segments.
.PP
Example:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]bofh.logjet\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]bofh-1.logjet\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]bofh-2.logjet\f[R]
.PP
Used only with the \f[C]replay\f[R] command.
.SS \f[C]--dest\f[R] \f[I]url-or-host:port\f[R]
.PP
Destination OTLP/HTTP collector for replay.
.PP
Used only with the \f[C]replay\f[R] command.
.PP
If a full \f[C]http://host:port/path\f[R] URL is given, replay uses that
exact path.
If only \f[C]host:port\f[R] is given, replay defaults to
\f[C]/v1/logs\f[R].
.SS \f[C]--source\f[R] \f[I]host:port\f[R]
.PP
Upstream \f[C]logjetd\f[R] replay listener for the \f[C]bridge\f[R]
command.
.PP
If omitted, \f[C]bridge\f[R] uses \f[C]upstream.replay\f[R] from
configuration.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.PP
Default configuration path:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
/etc/logjet.conf
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Important keys:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
output: buffer
buffer.size: 100
buffer.messages: 5000
buffer.keep: 1500
file.path: /var/lib/logjet
file.size: 10240
file.name: vehicle.logjet
collector.url: http://127.0.0.1:4318/v1/logs
collector.timeout-ms: 10000
upstream.replay: 10.0.0.15:7002
upstream.retry-ms: 1000
upstream.connect-timeout-ms: 5000
ingest.protocol: otlp-http
ingest.listen: 127.0.0.1:4318
replay.listen: 0.0.0.0:7002
replay.poll_ms: 250
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Rules:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]buffer.size\f[R] is measured in KiB
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]file.size\f[R] is measured in KiB
.IP \[bu] 2
set either \f[C]buffer.size\f[R] or \f[C]buffer.messages\f[R], never
both
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]buffer.keep\f[R] applies only to memory mode
.IP \[bu] 2
file mode always keeps all rotated files
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]ingest.protocol\f[R] currently supports \f[C]wire\f[R],
\f[C]otlp-http\f[R], and \f[C]otlp-grpc\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]collector.url\f[R] configures replay destination URL
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]collector.timeout-ms\f[R] configures replay and bridge socket
timeout in milliseconds
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]upstream.replay\f[R] configures the default bridge source
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]upstream.retry-ms\f[R] configures bridge reconnect delay
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[C]upstream.connect-timeout-ms\f[R] configures bridge source connect
timeout
.SH STORAGE MODES
.SS Buffer mode
.PP
In-memory ring behavior:
.IP \[bu] 2
first \f[C]buffer.keep\f[R] messages are retained in a permanent front
jar
.IP \[bu] 2
later messages form a rotating FIFO tail
.PP
Drain model:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
[kept messages][rotating tail]
\f[R]
.fi
.SS File mode
.PP
Append-only file behavior:
.IP \[bu] 2
write to \f[C]name.logjet\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
rotate to \f[C]name-1.logjet\f[R], \f[C]name-2.logjet\f[R], and so on
when \f[C]file.size\f[R] is exceeded
.IP \[bu] 2
old rotated files are kept
.SH CURRENT FEATURES
.IP \[bu] 2
OTLP/HTTP log ingest on \f[C]POST /v1/logs\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
OTLP/gRPC log ingest on the standard logs export service
.IP \[bu] 2
append-only \f[C].logjet\f[R] file output with size-based rotation
.IP \[bu] 2
in-memory ring buffering with \f[C]buffer.keep\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
replay listener for downstream consumers using the internal framed
protocol
.IP \[bu] 2
continuous bridge mode from replay listener to OTLP/HTTP collectors
.IP \[bu] 2
one-shot file replay to OTLP/HTTP collectors with
\f[C]logjetd replay\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
configurable replay destination via \f[C]collector.url\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
inspection of \f[C].logjet\f[R] files and directories
.SH CURRENT LIMITATIONS
.IP \[bu] 2
replay listener traffic is not OTLP
.IP \[bu] 2
persisted resume checkpoints and acknowledgements are not implemented
.IP \[bu] 2
slow-consumer handling is basic
.IP \[bu] 2
file mode does not delete old rotated files
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Run the daemon with explicit config:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
logjetd --config ./logjetd.conf
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Inspect generated files:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
logjetd inspect ./logs
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Replay files to a local collector:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
logjetd --config ./logjetd.conf replay --path ./logs --name bofh.logjet
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Bridge from a remote replay listener into a collector:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
logjetd --config ./logjetd.conf bridge --source 10.0.0.15:7002
\f[R]
.fi
